A||N||18||gkozlov||Inop Heater||||1||
Z||000000||gkozlov||08-06-2000||10:49 PM||||Its a 86 SE v6 with inop air and heat. Where do I begin looking to fix the heater? Winter is coming and I've been blowing it off all summer. Now its starting to get cool in the mornings. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sign1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Carrolles||08-07-2000||12:01 AM||||I need a little info.  When you say it doesn't work do you mean that the blower does not work or that the blower works but it does not blow hot air?<P>If the blower does not work at all start by checking out the fuse.  Next check to see that the fan motor is getting power.  The power leads are on the front of the blower motor which is located near the spare tire.  If there is still no power check the fan switch on the dash.<P>You can remove the fan and take it to the work bench and apply 12volt dc to the connector to see if the motor works.  If it doesn't then you probably need a new fan motor.  I recently bought one for my 89 Bonneville at AutoZone for around $25.00.  I believe it is the same motor or at least very similar.<P>If the fan works but the air never gets hot check to see that the heater door is opening.  Next check to see if the cooling system is low on coolant.  It is possible that an air bubble is trapped in the heater core.  If so fill the coolant system and burp it per the owner's manual.  (Do a search on Radiator for more info).<P>Also check to see that someone hasn't cut the heater hoses and bypassed the heater core.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000002||gkozlov||08-07-2000||05:33 AM||||Thanks, Ill try to check it out today. <BR>Oh yeah, it never gets hot.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sign1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||gkozlov||09-30-2000||02:38 PM||||The heater does not blow hot air. It is getting power. You mentioned removing the fan. I wouldn't know where to start with removing the fan or testing it. I also checked my antifreeze level and that is also good. Where is this heater door so I can check it. I am so lost. If anyone can guide me in the right direction I would appreciate it. gkozlov<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Carrolles:<BR><B>I need a little info.  When you say it doesn't work do you mean that the blower does not work or that the blower works but it does not blow hot air?<P>If the blower does not work at all start by checking out the fuse.  Next check to see that the fan motor is getting power.  The power leads are on the front of the blower motor which is located near the spare tire.  If there is still no power check the fan switch on the dash.<P>You can remove the fan and take it to the work bench and apply 12volt dc to the connector to see if the motor works.  If it doesn't then you probably need a new fan motor.  I recently bought one for my 89 Bonneville at AutoZone for around $25.00.  I believe it is the same motor or at least very similar.<P>If the fan works but the air never gets hot check to see that the heater door is opening.  Next check to see if the cooling system is low on coolant.  It is possible that an air bubble is trapped in the heater core.  If so fill the coolant system and burp it per the owner's manual.  (Do a search on Radiator for more info).<P>Also check to see that someone hasn't cut the heater hoses and bypassed the heater core.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sign1_3.jpg">  <BR><A HREF="http://g.kozlov.tripod.com/gkozlov.htm" TARGET=_blank>GKozlovs Fiero Site</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||09-30-2000||08:10 PM||||The air is moving it's just not hot. correct? It's not a fan problem.<P>Not heating is one of 2 things. broken temperature control or a heater core problem.<P>The easy way to check the temperature door is pull out the left speeker. then you should be able to see or feel the arm on top of the heater box that moves the door. have someone move the slide on the dash while you watch the arm.<P>If the door is moving ok then it's likely the heater core.<P>The heater core can plug up. Instructions to remove it are in the haynes book. (there are additional notes in my cave that help.)<P>If the heater core isn't blocked then one of the lines to it may be plugged or damaged.||||reg||1
Z||000005||gkozlov||09-30-2000||09:05 PM||||Ok, I checked the arm and it is moving. It must be the heater core. Ill look in the cave hopefully to find help on checking this. Thanks<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sign1_3.jpg">  <BR><A HREF="http://g.kozlov.tripod.com/gkozlov.htm" TARGET=_blank>GKozlovs Fiero Site</A>||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||09-30-2000||11:02 PM||||The simplest test for the core is to see if the heater hoses get hot. If the core is not flowing water then the hoses won't get hot either. (Or they won't get as hot as they should, which is about the same temp as the radiator hoses once the thermosat is open.)||||reg||1
Z||000007||gkozlov||09-30-2000||11:06 PM||||Heater hoses coming from where? Im not very mechanically enclined. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Carrolles||10-02-2000||10:25 AM||||There are two pipes that run under the car along the fuel tank that carry heated water to and from the heater.  They run partway up the engine firewall (between the engine and the seats).  If the engine has ever been pulled these could have been crushed.  Follow the heater hoses from the engine to these pipes and see if this has happened.||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||10-02-2000||11:36 AM||||You can also feel them up front in the spare tire bay and see if they get hot. they are by the washer fluid tank.||||reg||1
Z||000010||gkozlov||10-07-2000||02:12 PM||||OK, Im still confused. This time I got my camera. These are the pipes upfront. They are not getting hot. Are they supposed to?<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/A-B.jpg"> <BR>Also pipes labeled H seam to be cut. I only gently tugged on them and they came out. Could this be the problem?<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/H-I.jpg"> <BR>I couldn't see the temperature door. So I removed the heater core cover. <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/D.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/E.jpg"> <BR>Pic D being open and E being closed.<BR>I was told by a friend that if the heater core had gone bad it would leak. It does not.<P>You guys had also told me to check the heater hoses. They seam to heat up but its difficult to know for sure since they are insulated. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/F.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/G.jpg"> <BR>Sorry for the really long post. But I cant explain w/o use of pics. Please help. Its cold.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sign1_3.jpg">  <BR><A HREF="http://g.kozlov.tripod.com/gkozlov.htm" TARGET=_blank>GKozlovs Fiero Site</A>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Patrick||10-07-2000||03:08 PM||||<BR>I hope I'm not misinterpreting your pictures and descriptions, but if the two hoses labeled "H" have been cut, THAT is your problem. I bet the heater core leaks and the previous owner just bypassed it by cutting the hoses somewhere and connecting the two hoses that return to the engine.||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||10-07-2000||03:51 PM||||Yeah, H is them and being cut would do it. You need to get under the car and see what's going on. It could be a simple as the hose broke underneith and they looped it to close the leak. <P>Could be the heater core was leaking. Hard to tell from pictures but it looks like it was wet in there. Notice how, at least in the picture, the bottom of the core is discolored.<P>At this point the easiest thing to do is take the core the rest of the way out and run it by a radiator shop. they can leak test it. and tell you what if any thing is needed. That or just replace it for good measure. new ones are only about $50.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-07-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||gkozlov||10-08-2000||12:56 AM||||First of all, thank you. Can I use a heater core from any other car? I cant seam to find a heater core anywhere locally. Id like to get a new one. Are they all about the same?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sign1_3.jpg">   <BR><A HREF="http://g.kozlov.tripod.com/gkozlov.htm" TARGET=_blank>GKozlovs Fiero Site</A><p>[This message has been edited by gkozlov (edited 10-08-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||gkozlov||10-08-2000||01:39 PM||||up||||reg||1
Z||000015||StuGood||10-08-2000||02:12 PM||||What's "up?"<P>Couldn't resist...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><B>Somebody</B> in a town the size of Huntsville <B>has</B> to have a replacement heater core....even Autozone carries them.||||reg||5
Z||000016||gkozlov||10-08-2000||04:23 PM||||According to autozone no other(replacement) heatercore will work. It has to be Fiero specific. I have a hard time believing that. Im hoping a heatercore from possibly another GM will work.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sign1_3.jpg">  <BR><A HREF="http://g.kozlov.tripod.com/gkozlov.htm" TARGET=_blank>GKozlovs Fiero Site</A>||||reg||1
Z||000017||theogre||10-08-2000||05:31 PM||||Don't waste time on used heater cores.<P>You can get new ones for about 50-60 dollars. You can get them thru any parts store carparts.com or wrenchead.com||||reg||1
Z||000018||gkozlov||10-08-2000||05:55 PM||||I dont want a used one. Its just that I cant find a new one locally. I was hoping I could buy one from another GM model car that would work||||reg||1
